.\" Original maux page, MIT, 2026-09-21.
.\" Historical-source decision: cloned from GNO/ME's chtyp(1) (Greg
.\" Thompson), mechanism not spelling -- no Research UNIX or early BSD
.\" counterpart exists, ProDOS types having no Unix equivalent.
.\" Rendered by make man-pages; staged on play image in /usr/man/.
.TH CHTYP 1 "September 21, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
chtyp \- change a file's ProDOS type and auxiliary type
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chtyp
.RB [ \-t
.IR type ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR aux ]
.I file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Set the ProDOS file type, the auxiliary type, or both.
At least one of the two options is required; naming neither is an error
rather than a command that rewrites each file with its own current
values.
.PP
The type is not decoration.
On ProDOS it is where "can this be run?" lives, and this system loads a
program at the address in its auxiliary type, so a binary built here
needs both set before it will run.
.TP
.B \-t type
The file type, written as a decimal number, a
.B $
followed by hexadecimal digits, or one of the mnemonics
.BR TXT ,
.BR BIN ,
.BR DIR ,
.BR SYS ,
.BR BAS ,
.BR INT ,
.BR VAR ,
.BR REL ,
.BR RELM ,
.BR PAS ,
.BR ATK ,
.BR S16 .
Mnemonics may be typed in either case.
A value that does not fit in one byte is refused.
.TP
.B \-a aux
The auxiliary type, as a decimal or
.B $
hexadecimal number from 0 to 65535.
.PP
Everything else the directory entry holds \- the access byte, the
modification date and time, the length \- is read before the change and
written back unaltered.
.SH EXAMPLES
Stamp an assembled program so it can be run:
.PP
.B chtyp -t BIN -a '$0800' MYPROG
.PP
Mark a file as text again after editing it as data:
.PP
.B chtyp -t TXT NOTES
.SH FILES
/usr/man/CHTYP  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR chmod (1),
.IR file (1),
.IR ls (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if every named file was changed, 1 if any file could
not be read or written, and 2 for a usage error.
A file that cannot be reached is reported and the remaining files are
still changed.
.SH BUGS
A
.B $
must be quoted, because the shell would otherwise expand it:
write
.B '$F8'
or
.BR \e$F8 .
An unquoted
.B $F8
usually reaches this program as nothing at all, which is a usage error
here rather than a silent change to type zero.
.PP
There is no
.B \-l
option to stamp a file for a language.
GNO/ME's version has one, naming ORCA and APW compilers; those are
auxiliary-type conventions of a toolchain that does not exist on this
machine.
